  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 AM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Potential for strong storms with heavy downpours \ntoday and additional localized heavy rain on Tuesday. \n\nA cold front will be the primary driver for the aforementioned \nweather and hazards today and Tuesday.  That front will drift south \ntoward and across the mountains later today. Mostly sunny conditions \nthis morning will give way to increasing clouds and scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms this afternoon and early evening, with the \nhighest coverage in our CWA near the VA/NC border where a lingering \noutflow boundary and pre-frontal convergence zone may set up. \nDespite stronger shear remaining to our north, adequate heating and \nmoisture ahead of the front will result in moderate instability and \nthus could support a few strong to severe storms with damaging wind \ngusts the primary threat. Most of this activity should be confined \nto areas west of US-1. Heavy rainfall could also produce localized \nflash flooding, especially in and around the Triad where urban areas \nmay be more vulnerable to excessive rainfall.  \n\nThe front will push south across NC Tuesday, placing our area on the \ncooler side of the boundary and largely ending the severe weather \nthreat for Tuesday. However, rain will likely continue with locally \nhigh rainfall rates possible along the front. The entire area \nremains under a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall \nwith the greatest concern Tuesday afternoon. Localized flooding will \nbe possible where heavier or repeated rainfall occurs.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Cold air damming will bring much below normal \ntemperatures during mid to late week. \n\nAs the surface front settles S of NC, confidence is high in the \ndevelopment of a robust, classical cold air damming event this week. \nWith an early-week rex block in place over the Upper Midwest and \nManitoba/Ontario, mid level energy will undercut the blocking high, \ncrossing IN/OH before deepening to a low over the Mid Atlantic \nregion during the mid-late week. This will keep the surface frontal \nzone pinned to our S and SE, with steady advection of cool and low-\ndewpoint surface air from a fairly strong and anchored surface high \ncentered over Quebec. This high will weaken as it slowly retrogrades \nto the WSW late Thu into Fri, drifting into the N Great Lakes and S \nOntario in response to what is expected to be strong coastal \ncyclogenesis just off the Mid Atlantic and Northeast coast by \nFri/Sat, but the circulation around such a low alone should maintain \nthe wedge regime and low level CAA through at least Fri, despite the \nlikelihood of further drying aloft potentially eroding much of the \nCAD stratus by late in the day. The result will be multiple days of \nhigh temps 10-15 deg F below normal, including highs from the \nTriangle to the N and W in the 60s to around 70 each day Wed through \nFri. These temps will be further held down by copious cloud cover \nand widespread overrunning rain, driven in large part by moist \nisentropic upglide focused at 295K-305K from Wed morning through Thu \nnight. In addition to the cool days, we're likely to see a few \nnighttime lows in the 40s near the VA border Thu night through Sat \nnight. Rain chances are expected to dwindle Fri and remain low into \nthe weekend, as low-level stability decreases with the weakening and \nretrograding surface ridge. But the potential for a baggy mid level \ntrough or shear axis holding across the region along with a \npersistent low level flow from the N or NNW around the coastal low \nshould result in highs remaining at least a category below normal \nSat/Sun. Given that we've seen above to much above-normal temps for \nall but a couple of days so far this month, several days of below to \nmuch-below normal temps will be a welcome respite for many.",
